# Illustrative log excerpt — reconstructed from the field values and query logic
# documented in this lab's README (Stage 2). Field names and event structure
# match real Windows Security Event Log / Splunk output for Event ID 4769;
# this file is provided as an annotated teaching example, not a raw export.

EventCode=4769
_time=2026-08-08T02:14:07
Account_Name=swetha@LAB.LOCAL
Service_Name=svc-sql
Client_Address=::ffff:192.168.56.107
Ticket_Encryption_Type=0x17          # <-- RC4 (0x17). Modern domains should only issue AES (0x11/0x12). RC4 = crackable offline.
Ticket_Options=0x40810000
Failure_Code=0x0

# --- Why this is suspicious ---
# 1. Ticket_Encryption_Type=0x17 (RC4) is the key indicator — this domain
#    supports AES, so an RC4 ticket suggests the requester deliberately
#    downgraded the request (a hallmark of Impacket's GetUserSPNs / Rubeus).
# 2. Client_Address (192.168.56.107) is the Kali attacker VM's IP in this lab,
#    not a normal domain-joined endpoint.
# 3. A single request like this is investigatable but low-confidence alone;
#    production tuning adds `| where count > 5` to catch bulk/automated
#    SPN spraying rather than one-off renewals. See detections/stage2_kerberoasting.spl.
